5. Task 4 — ContrarianAgentNode (component_id = "contrarian-agent")

New file apps/engine/app/nodes/intelligence_agents/contrarian.py, also subclassing LlmAgentNode. This one is structurally different from every other agent — it consumes an upstream Signal, not raw market/news data:

python
class ContrarianAgentNode(LlmAgentNode):
    component_id = "contrarian-agent"
    default_provider_id = "anthropic"
    default_model_id = "claude-haiku"        # confirm exact id against mock/models.ts
    default_system_prompt = (
        "You are a contrarian analyst. Your job is to identify when another agent's "
        "conviction may be overextended — excessive confidence is often a late-stage "
        "signal, not a strong one. You do not automatically oppose every signal; you "
        "only push back when the incoming conviction looks crowded or overconfident. "
        "Absent that condition, defer to the incoming view rather than manufacturing "
        "disagreement for its own sake."
    )

compute_deterministic_baseline:

Find the first upstream output with type == "Signal" — reuse self.find_upstream(ctx, "Signal"), which the base class already provides (per your Step Zero audit finding #3, this requires zero base-class changes). If none exists: standard "no data" fallback (applied_rule: "Default Flat (No Data)" — but phrase the rationale/reasoning specifically as "No upstream directional signal to evaluate", since "no data" here means something structurally different than it does for technical-agent — a contrarian agent's "no data" is "nothing to contest", not "no market features available"; make this distinction clear in the returned text, not just internally).
If found, read incoming_direction = signal["direction"], incoming_confidence = signal.get("confidence", 0.5).
Fade logic: only take a contrarian (opposite-direction) position when incoming confidence exceeds a high bar — fade_threshold = cfg.get("fadeThreshold", 0.85). If incoming_confidence >= fade_threshold and incoming_direction != "flat": take the opposite direction, with a confidence that is deliberately moderate, not equally extreme (e.g. min(0.70, 0.40 + (incoming_confidence - fade_threshold) * 2)) — a contrarian call should never claim higher conviction than the very consensus it's fading, since that would be internally incoherent; applied_rule: "Contrarian_Fade_Overextended_Conviction (>= 0.85)".
If incoming confidence is below the fade threshold, or direction is flat: defer — return direction: incoming_direction (i.e., agree, don't manufacture contrarian noise) with a modestly reduced confidence relative to the incoming signal (e.g. incoming_confidence * 0.8), applied_rule: "Contrarian_Defers_No_Crowding_Detected", and a rationale that says plainly it found no evidence of overextension and is not overriding the incoming view. This matters: a contrarian agent that always opposes is just a sign-flipper, not a genuine "fade the crowd" heuristic, and the system prompt above explicitly instructs the LLM path the same way — the deterministic baseline must model the same discipline the prompt asks the LLM for, or the two paths would contradict each other's stated philosophy.

build_feature_summary: {"incoming_direction": ..., "incoming_confidence": ..., "incoming_rationale": signal.get("rationale", ""), "close": current_close} — give the LLM the upstream agent's own stated reasoning as context, not just its number, so it can genuinely evaluate whether that reasoning sounds crowded/overextended versus well-founded.

